  "throughline": "The *Discourse on the Method* is the book in which modern philosophy decides where to stand. [René Descartes](/philosophy/thinker/descartes) published it in 1637, anonymously, in French rather than Latin, as the preface to three scientific essays, and what it argues is not a doctrine about the world but a procedure for reaching one. He tells the story of his own dissatisfaction with everything he was taught, lays out four plain rules for conducting reason, sets up a temporary moral code so he can live while he tears his beliefs down, and then does the tearing down: he doubts everything that can be doubted until one thing refuses to fall, the fact that he is thinking. From that single point, *I think, therefore I am*, he tries to rebuild knowledge of God, the soul, and the physical world, and he ends by proposing that a properly conducted science could make human beings the \"lords and possessors of nature.\" It is short, personal, and written so a non-specialist could follow it, and it relocated the foundation of knowledge (what a person can justifiably build every other belief on top of) from the authority of the ancients to the thinking of a single mind.",
