    "In 1637 a book appeared in Leiden, in the Netherlands, with no author's name on it. It was printed by Jan Maire, written in French rather than the Latin that serious philosophy used, and it was not even sold on its own. It was the preface to three scientific essays: one on optics (*La Dioptrique*), one on weather and the rainbow (*Les Météores*), and one on geometry (*La Géométrie*) that founded the coordinate system schoolchildren still call Cartesian. The preface was the *Discourse on the Method of Rightly Conducting One's Reason and of Seeking Truth in the Sciences*, and its author was [René Descartes](/philosophy/thinker/descartes).",
