      "Before the *Discourse*, natural philosophy was a Latin profession. A serious book in the genre was addressed to clergy and scholars who had been through the university curriculum, and it argued by citing authorities: Aristotle above all (the ancient Greek philosopher whose works ruled the universities), then Aquinas (the great medieval Christian theologian), Averroes (the Islamic philosopher whose commentaries carried Aristotle into Europe), and the rest of the inherited canon. A position was defended by showing it agreed with the great names and could survive a syllogism (a formal logical argument that draws a conclusion from two accepted premises). French-language books existed, but scholarly ones did not speak to the ordinary educated reader. The order of business was settled too. You began from the authoritative content, the body of received learning, and used logical method to organize and defend it. This was a coherent and genuinely impressive system, and it had held the field for centuries.",
      "The *Discourse* breaks with it on every axis at once, and the combination is what was new. It is written in French, for the unspecialized reader, which is a claim about who philosophy belongs to. It is autobiographical, presenting its method as something one man arrived at by living and thinking rather than as doctrine handed down, so that the authority is first-person experience and not tradition. Most consequential of all, it reverses the order of inquiry. Before stating anything about what exists, Descartes asks what procedure could possibly yield certainty, and only then applies it. The method comes first; the content is whatever survives the method. That is the exact opposite of starting from authoritative content and organizing it.",
