          "p": "The *Discourse* opens with a joke that is also its premise. \"Good sense is, of all things among men, the most equally distributed; for every one thinks himself so abundantly provided with it, that those even who are the most difficult to satisfy in everything else, do not usually desire a larger measure of this quality than they already possess.\" Good sense, in Descartes's vocabulary, is just reason; he uses \"good sense\" and \"reason\" interchangeably through the book. The line is funny because of what it implies. Everyone is satisfied with the amount of reason they have, which is to say nobody thinks the problem is theirs, and yet people reach wildly different conclusions from the same evidence. If the raw faculty really is evenly handed out, then the difference between good and bad thinking cannot be a difference of equipment. It has to be a difference of use."
