          "p": "Why narrate it as a personal story at all? Because Descartes is careful not to set himself up as a new authority issuing commands. He frames Part I as a confession, the account of paths he himself followed, offered as an example rather than a prescription. The autobiographical voice does real philosophical work here. A man who has just argued that no one should accept things on authority cannot very well demand that the reader accept his method on his authority. So he shows his work instead, telling where the method came from in his own life, and invites the reader to judge it by their own reason. The form matches the content: this is a book against deferring to the great names, written by a man who refuses to become one."
