          "p": "The method comes down to four rules, which Descartes presents in a single passage. The first is the rule of certainty: \"never to accept anything for true which I did not clearly know to be such; that is to say, carefully to avoid precipitancy and prejudice, and to comprise nothing more in my judgment than what was presented to my mind so clearly and distinctly as to exclude all ground of doubt.\" (\"Precipitancy\" in Veitch's older English just means rushing to a conclusion before there is reason to.) Accept nothing you cannot see clearly and distinctly to be true, and so rule out both haste and inherited prejudice. \"Clearly and distinctly\" is Descartes's own technical standard, and it carries the weight of everything that follows: an idea is clear and distinct when it is so sharp and self-evident that the mind cannot doubt it even if it tries, the way 2 plus 2 making 4 is not merely familiar but impossible to genuinely call into question. The second is the rule of analysis: \"to divide each of the difficulties under examination into as many parts as possible, and as might be necessary for its adequate solution.\" Break every hard problem into its smallest pieces. The third is the rule of order, building from the simple to the complex: \"to conduct my thoughts in such order that, by commencing with objects the simplest and easiest to know, I might ascend by little and little, and, as it were, step by step, to the knowledge of the more complex.\" The fourth is the rule of review: \"in every case to make enumerations so complete, and reviews so general, that I might be assured that nothing was omitted.\" Check everything, leave nothing out."
