          "p": "There is an obvious problem with the plan of Part II, and Descartes faces it directly in Part III. A man cannot suspend all his beliefs and still get through a Tuesday. Tearing down the whole house of one's opinions takes time, and in the meantime a person has to eat, work, keep promises, and make choices, none of which can wait for certainty to be reestablished. A house under demolition is not somewhere to live. So before he begins doubting, Descartes builds himself a temporary shelter: a code of conduct, explicitly labeled provisional, to govern his actions while his beliefs are in pieces. The word provisional is his own and it matters. He is not deriving a true morality from the method; he is borrowing reasonable rules to live by until the method has done its work, the way a builder rents a place to stay while the house comes down and goes back up."
